    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "After the Charles River Railroad opened in the 1850s, Newton Centre shifted from a small agricultural crossroads into an affluent railroad suburb.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.newtonma.gov/government/historic-newton/research/newton-history/villages/newton-centre"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The 1890 Newton Centre Station became the main arrival point for commuters, cementing the village as the city’s activity hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://boston.wiki/a/Newton_Centre"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The village’s main commercial heart is a triangle formed by Beacon, Centre, and Langley streets.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newton_Centre%2C_Massachusetts"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Crystal Lake, a 33‑acre natural lake, is a popular spot for local swimming and recreation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newton_Centre%2C_Massachusetts"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Highlands Station (1885), the express building (1886), and the main station (1890) show five years of focused transportation investment.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
